WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.334
The ASP.

2
00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:05.068
NET Core request pipeline is a security control surface.

3
00:00:05.068 --> 00:00:12.938
Every request flows through middleware before it reaches an endpoint, and each component can change what happens next.

4
00:00:12.938 --> 00:00:28.410
Routing, HTTPS redirection, exception handling, authentication, authorization, endpoint mapping, static files, CORS, antiforgery behavior, and custom middleware should be understood as part of one flow rather than isolated settings.

5
00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:33.345
Middleware order matters because some decisions depend on earlier context.

6
00:00:33.345 --> 00:00:38.747
Routing must identify endpoint metadata before endpoint authorization can use it.

7
00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:44.282
Authentication must establish the user before authorization evaluates access rules.

8
00:00:44.282 --> 00:00:49.617
Exception handling must produce controlled responses without exposing internals.

9
00:00:49.617 --> 00:00:55.619
Endpoint mapping must not create unexpected paths around the protections the team expects.

10
00:00:55.619 --> 00:00:58.821
Development behavior should stay in development.

11
00:00:58.821 --> 00:01:13.426
Detailed error pages, verbose diagnostics, local identity assumptions, permissive origins, relaxed certificate behavior, or test-only endpoints can help during local work, but they should not become production behavior.

12
00:01:13.426 --> 00:01:25.563
Production should use controlled errors, secure transport expectations, explicit endpoint protection, and logging that helps operators without revealing sensitive details to callers.

13
00:01:25.563 --> 00:01:29.831
Secure transport assumptions also belong in the pipeline review.

14
00:01:29.831 --> 00:01:36.834
Applications commonly sit behind reverse proxies, load balancers, ingress controllers, or cloud services.

15
00:01:36.834 --> 00:01:47.037
The application should be configured so it understands forwarded headers, HTTPS expectations, and redirect behavior in the actual deployment environment.

16
00:01:47.037 --> 00:01:52.706
Otherwise a setting that worked locally may not match how production traffic arrives.

17
00:01:52.706 --> 00:01:55.240
Endpoint protection should be visible.

18
00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:05.644
MVC controllers, Razor Pages, Minimal APIs, endpoint groups, health checks, static assets, and administrative surfaces should have intentional access rules.

19
00:02:05.644 --> 00:02:16.314
Reviewers should be able to tell which endpoints are public, which require authentication, which require policies, and which are limited to operational systems.

20
00:02:16.314 --> 00:02:23.517
The practical review question is, what happens to a request from arrival to endpoint execution and response.

21
00:02:23.517 --> 00:02:28.852
If the team cannot describe that path, security behavior may depend on accident.

22
00:02:28.852 --> 00:02:37.788
A coordinated request pipeline makes access decisions, error behavior, transport assumptions, and routing easier to test and maintain.
